course: dictionary
lesson: 4785
title: Hullo, an old greeting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hullo is another spelling of hello. It is old-fashioned, and you will often find it in old British books. Animals in classic stories greet each other with «Hullo!» as a friendly welcome.
explain: Hullo can also show surprise. When a character sees something unexpected, they may cry «Hullo, what's this?» The words around it tell you the feeling. Ask yourself: is someone being greeted, or is someone surprised?
explain: Look at the context. «Hullo, mother!» is a greeting said to someone you know. «Hullo, washerwoman!» is a greeting said by a fox to a woman. The word is the same, but the sentence tells you what it is doing.
fact: Hullo is another spelling of hello.
fact: Hallo is another spelling of hello too.
fact: «Hullo» can be used to greet someone, or to show surprise.
fact: Hullo is old-fashioned and is common in old British books.
fact: In «Hullo, hullo!» a cheerful character often notices something.
exercise: A fox says «Hullo, washerwoman!» What is the fox doing? | Shouting at a thief | Singing a song | Asking for food | *Greeting the washerwoman
exercise: In an old book, a cheerful character says «Hullo, hullo!» on seeing something. What does this often show? | That they are very angry | That they are lost | That they are going to sleep | *That they are happy and have noticed something
exercise: Hullo is another spelling of which word? | Yellow | Goodbye | *Hello | Hurry
test: In «Hullo, mother!», what is the speaker doing? | *Greeting her mother | Asking for money | Waving goodbye | Frightening her mother
test: Hullo is old-fashioned. Where would you most often find it? | In a science textbook about rockets | In a modern text message from space | On a road sign | *In old British books
exam: A cheerful character cries «Hullo, hullo!» on seeing something new. What does this show? | Fear | Anger | Tiredness | *A cheerful greeting or surprise
